    "text": "Finding: Mozart effect: original finding small and short-lived; meta-analysis finds little specific effect, explained by arousal and mood.\nSources: Rauscher, Shaw & Ky (1993), Music and spatial task performance, Nature 365, 611 (36 college students, 10-15 minute effect). Pietschnig, Voracek & Formann (2010), Mozart effect-Shmozart effect: a meta-analysis, Intelligence 38, 314-323 (about 40 studies, over 3,000 participants). Thompson, Schellenberg & Husain (2001), Psychological Science. Georgia CD program: Hetland (2000), J. Aesthetic Education, describes Governor Zell Miller's 1998 initiative.\nConfidence: HIGH that the popular claim is unsupported. The meta-analysis abstract was checked: d about 0.37 for Mozart vs silence and about the same for other music, with publication bias and a lab-affiliation effect."
