    "text": "Finding: Enriched-environment rat studies show deprivation effects rather than enrichment benefits; the 'enriched' cage approximates normal rat life.\nSources: Rosenzweig, Bennett & Diamond (1972), Brain changes in response to experience, Scientific American. Greenough's caveat: Greenough, Black & Wallace (1987) and Black & Greenough in later chapters (e.g., Black 2003, Environment and development of the nervous system, in Handbook of Psychology) explicitly describe the complex cage as closer to the wild and the standard cage as deprived. Bruer (1999) makes the same point.\nConfidence: HIGH for the rat findings; MODERATE to HIGH for the interpretation, which is Greenough's own and widely accepted in the field."
