    "text": "Finding: Meta-analysis of 128 experiments: expected tangible rewards undermined free-choice intrinsic motivation, more for children; positive feedback enhanced it.\nSources: Deci, Koestner & Ryan (1999), Psychological Bulletin, DOI 10.1037/0033-2909.125.6.627. Counterpoint: Cameron & Pierce (1994), Review of Educational Research, DOI 10.3102/00346543064003363 (negative effects limited to expected tangible rewards for simply doing a task); Bardach & Murayama (2024), Learning and Instruction, DOI 10.1016/j.learninstruc.2024.102056 (rewards as an entry point).\nConfidence: HIGH that expected tangible rewards for enjoyed activities can lower interest; the size and generality are debated, which the script says."
