    "text": "Finding: Introducing a story stem increased 4-year-olds' pretend play complexity with their mothers.\nSources: Stuart, Stougaard, Smith-Nielsen & Væver (2023), Scandinavian Journal of Psychology, DOI 10.1111/sjop.12920 (N = 102). The storm example illustrates a story stem.\nConfidence: MODERATE.\n\nFinding: Advice to stay in character and avoid quizzing.\nSources: Application of the findings above; directive goal-setting by parents reduced later engagement in a related play study (Sobel et al., 2021, Developmental Science, DOI 10.1111/desc.13057).\nConfidence: MODERATE. Presented as advice.\n\nFinding: Play tutoring (adults joining to support roles and stories) vs role-play materials vs control, 27 Swiss playgroups, 211 children: tutoring improved pretend competence, behavioral skills and peer relationships (educator report); no effect on social-cognitive or emotional skills.\nSources: Jaggy, Kalkusch, Bossi, Weiss, Sticca & Perren (2023), The impact of social pretend play on preschoolers' social development, Early Childhood Research Quarterly, DOI 10.1016/j.ecresq.2023.01.012.\nConfidence: MODERATE-HIGH. Randomized; teacher-reported outcomes."
