    "text": "Finding: Same children at 16 months: broader, bilateral prefrontal regions recruited, with no change in task performance; the authors note the need for replication. At 3.5 years, performance was significantly improved (the abstract gives no size, so the script says clearly better).\nSources: Fiske et al. (2024), Imaging Neuroscience, DOI 10.1162/imag_a_00206; Fiske et al. (2025), Developmental Cognitive Neuroscience, DOI 10.1016/j.dcn.2025.101557.\nConfidence: MODERATE-HIGH. One group of children; the script passes on the authors' own caution.\n\nFinding: Steady improvement on the touchscreen task at 18, 21 and 24 months (38 children, followed over time) and between 24 and 30 months (81 children, compared across ages).\nSources: Holmboe et al. (2021), The early childhood inhibitory touchscreen task, PLoS ONE, DOI 10.1371/journal.pone.0260695.\nConfidence: HIGH.\n\nFinding: 113 infants at 10 and 16 months: little stability of individual differences from 10 to 16 months; measures of similar skills hung together only weakly at 10 months (r .09 to .35) and somewhat better at 16 months (r .23 to .59).\nSources: Hendry et al. (2021), Developmental Science, DOI 10.1111/desc.13193.\nConfidence: HIGH."
