    "text": "Finding: 40 mothers and 13-month-olds filmed at home: the amount, diversity, functions and content of maternal language differed systematically across feeding, grooming, book sharing, object play and transitions.\nSources: Tamis-LeMonda et al. (2019), Routine Language: Speech Directed to Infants During Home Activities, Child development, DOI 10.1111/cdev.13089.\nConfidence: HIGH.\n\nFinding: 63 mothers with children aged 18 to 29 months in four settings (mealtime, dressing, book reading, toy play): setting had main effects on mothers' child-directed speech.\nSources: Hoff-Ginsberg (1991), Mother-child conversation in different social classes and communicative settings, Child development, DOI 10.1111/j.1467-8624.1991.tb01569.x.\nConfidence: HIGH.\n\nFinding: 100 picture books contained more unique word types than a matched sample of child-directed speech.\nSources: Montag et al. (2015), The words children hear: Picture books and the statistics for language learning, Psychological science, DOI 10.1177/0956797615594361.\nConfidence: HIGH."
