    "text": "Finding: Parental singing and the home music environment were associated with word comprehension, gestures and (in one study) word production in infants and young toddlers.\nSources: Franco et al. (2021), Singing to infants matters: Early singing interactions affect musical preferences and facilitate vocabulary building, Journal of Child Language, DOI 10.1017/s0305000921000167. Papadimitriou et al. (2021), The impact of the home musical environment on infants' language development, Infant behavior & development, DOI 10.1016/j.infbeh.2021.101651. Boyne et al. (2025), Parental social and musical characteristics, the home music environment, and child language development in infancy, Infancy : the official journal of the International Society on Infant Studies, DOI 10.1111/infa.70008.\nConfidence: LOW to MEDIUM. Three small correlational studies (26 to 64 families), mostly under 18 months, based on parent report. The narration calls the research thin."
