    "text": "Finding: 276 toddlers aged 16 to 21 months, assessed weekly for six weeks, in urban Germany, urban India, rural India and rural Nso families: mirror self-recognition increased with age everywhere and was higher in the autonomy-supporting urban contexts; best explained by caretakers' emphasis on autonomous socialization goals.\nSources: Kärtner et al. (2012), The development of mirror self-recognition in different sociocultural contexts, Monographs of the Society for Research in Child Development, DOI 10.1111/j.1540-5834.2012.00688.x.\nConfidence: HIGH. The abstract names the Nso but not the country. Confirmed in the second research pass: two studies by the same research group describe their sample as Cameroonian Nso farmers (Keller et al. (2005), Parenting styles and the development of the categorical self: A longitudinal study on mirror self-recognition in Cameroonian Nso and German families, International Journal of Behavioral Development, DOI 10.1177/01650250500147485.)."
