    "text": "Finding: SECOND RESEARCH PASS (v2), slide 13. Typical children in four age groups (2, 3, 4 and 5 years); parents recorded noncompliance at home for two weeks. Virtually all (37 of 40) met the DSM-5 criterion for symptomatic levels of noncompliance; the drop in home noncompliance came at age 5. The authors lament the lack of normative observational data.\nSources: Livesay et al. (2020), The Dualistic Role of Child Noncompliance: Normal Developmental Process and Indicator of Child Psychopathology, Journal of Psychopathology and Behavioral Assessment, DOI 10.1007/s10862-019-09781-8.\nConfidence: HIGH for the numbers. The sample covers ages 2 to 5, not toddlers alone, and the narration says 'forty children' without an age. 'Refusing is just as typical' is my summary. The narration avoids naming the diagnosis."
