    "text": "Finding: Nationally representative US sample of 991 parents: by child age 2, 90% reported one or more forms of psychological aggression in the past year; severe forms 10% to 20% for toddlers.\nSources: Straus et al. (2003), Psychological Aggression by American Parents: National Data on Prevalence, Chronicity, and Severity, Journal of Marriage and Family, DOI 10.1111/j.1741-3737.2003.00795.x.\nConfidence: HIGH for prevalence; the category psychological aggression includes shouting, threats and name-calling, which the narration renders as harsh words or threats.\n\nFinding: 865 caregivers of toddlers (mean 27 months) in 14 countries: total and internalizing problems predicted by shouting, hitting and taking privileges; externalizing problems predicted by shouting and taking privileges.\nSources: Sehic et al. (2026), Links between Discipline Practices and Early Childhood Behavior Problems: Universal or Culture-Specific?, Child psychiatry and human development, DOI 10.1007/s10578-026-02079-3.\nConfidence: MODERATE. Cross-sectional parent report across countries."
