    "text": "Finding: 16,916 UK children at ages 3, 5 and 7: harsh discipline (smacking, shouting, telling off) related to trajectories of conduct, hyperactivity and emotional problems in high- and low-risk families.\nSources: Flouri et al. (2016), Environmental Adversity and Children’s Early Trajectories of Problem Behavior: The Role of Harsh Parental Discipline, Journal of Family Psychology, DOI 10.1037/fam0000258.\nConfidence: HIGH for the association; correlational, parent-reported.\n\nFinding: 229,465 households with children under 5 across 60 countries: psychological and physical aggression associated with more child aggression and distraction; no form of aggression benefited children in any country; shouting used by 66%.\nSources: Ward et al. (2023), Associations between 11 parental discipline behaviours and child outcomes across 60 countries, BMJ Open, DOI 10.1136/bmjopen-2021-058439.\nConfidence: HIGH for breadth; cross-sectional."
