    "text": "Finding: Parenting and tantrums run both ways (child effects on parenting); in the 94-toddler study the direction ran from parent to child.\nSources: Mo et al. (2022), Parental Discipline Techniques and Changes in Observed Temper Tantrum Severity in Toddlers, Research on Child and Adolescent Psychopathology, DOI 10.1007/s10802-022-01007-y.\nConfidence: HIGH. Taught in Lesson 4.3; one clause here.\n\nFinding: 177 mothers of school-aged children: proclivity to apologize associated with involvement, positive parenting and child prosocial behavior, and negatively with inconsistent discipline and child internalizing.\nSources: Lee et al. (2023), Associations between maternal apology, parenting, and child internalizing, externalizing and prosocial behaviors, Journal of Applied Developmental Psychology, DOI 10.1016/j.appdev.2022.101484.\nConfidence: LOW to MODERATE. Maternal self-report, school age, correlational, which the narration says."
