    "text": "Finding: 242 toddlers (mean 28.8 months): maternal emotion-dismissing style negatively, and coaching style positively, associated with toddlers' emotional competence; language ability also positively associated.\nSources: Ornaghi et al. (2019), The contribution of emotion knowledge, language ability, and maternal emotion socialization style to explaining toddlers’ emotion regulation, Social Development, DOI 10.1111/sode.12351.\nConfidence: MODERATE. Parent-report measures, cross-sectional; emotion coaching itself is Preschool 1.3's.\n\nFinding: Emotion coaching's three steps (accept, name, talk once calm) and the Tuning in to Kids trial.\nSources: Preschool class, Lesson 1.3, slides 10 to 15 (callback).\nConfidence: HIGH as a callback.\n\nFinding: 167 parents of children under 12, randomized: a self-compassion prompt after recalling a guilt- or shame-provoking parenting event raised self-compassion and lowered guilt and shame relative to control.\nSources: Sirois et al. (2018), Self-compassion Improves Parental Well-being in Response to Challenging Parenting Events, The Journal of Psychology, DOI 10.1080/00223980.2018.1523123.\nConfidence: MODERATE. Online experiment, immediate self-report outcome."
