    "text": "Finding: Meta-analysis of 13 trials of parenting interventions with self-compassion components: increased self-compassion and decreased parental depression, anxiety and stress pre to post; low methodological quality, mixed child outcomes, publication bias.\nSources: Jefferson et al. (2020), Parenting Self-compassion: a Systematic Review and Meta-analysis, Mindfulness, DOI 10.1007/s12671-020-01401-x.\nConfidence: MODERATE. The narration gives the caveats.\n\nFinding: 106 parents of children aged 1.3 to 9.3 years: children's difficulties related to parental guilt only when parental reflective functioning was not high.\nSources: Shalev et al. (2023), Parental guilt and children's internalizing and externalizing behavior: The moderating role of parental reflective functioning, Journal of family psychology : JFP : journal of the Division of Family Psychology of the American Psychological Association, DOI 10.1037/fam0001156.\nConfidence: MODERATE. Interview-coded guilt, cross-sectional; the narration paraphrases as reflected least on what was happening in the child's mind.\n\nFinding: Tantrums fade: prevalence below 50% by 5 to 6.\nSources: Toddler 4.1 slide 4 (callback to the review chapter).\nConfidence: HIGH as a callback."
